 ## Changelog
+- `done` LOD rewrite: hand-over + matching look (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, +1 test `hand_over_and_colours` not run; reads Distant Horizons' `BlazeDhTerrainRenderer` / far-fade renderers, whose GLSL is not in the source tree). Same quadtree, worker, mesher and generator; what changed is how it looks next to the chunks (screenshot: bright flat-colour LOD against dark textured chunks, a hard ring between them):
+    - Colours = mean of the real `terrain.png` top tile (`atlas::lod_color`, tint baked in) instead of the flat `block_color`; sun term = the chunk shader's (`mix(0.75, 1, sqrt(N.L))`, unshadowed); per-block brightness jitter fading 48..200 blocks (DH noise texture + `noiseDropoff`).
+    - Seam: the hard `discard` ring is replaced by a dither band (DH `uDitherDhRendering`): chunk shader and LOD shader use the same `ign` and complementary tests over `lod::fade_band(RENDER_DIST)` = (24, 40) blocks at 4 chunks. Only `terrain.png` texels fade (items and mobs do not). Off under water (`set_fog` fade 0).
+    - UNVERIFIED: that it compiles; the look on a device; band width (it must stay inside the radius the ring always covers, asserted for rings 2..11); LOD is still the seed's terrain (no trees, edits, caves), so the band shows that difference.
 - `done` distant terrain / LOD (port of the idea of Distant Horizons 3.3.4, read from its jar; written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, +3 tests in `render/lod.rs` not run, no FPS or timing numbers):
     - `render/lod.rs` (new): quadtree of tiles, 16 x 16 cells each, level `l` = cells of `4 << l` blocks (3 levels, 64/128/256-block tiles), radius `RADIUS` = 1024 blocks. A node splits while the eye is closer than 2 x its width; a node whose four children are not all ready is drawn itself, so streaming leaves no hole; missing tiles are requested coarse first, nearest first, 4 in flight, one `lod-gen` thread. Tiles unused for 600 frames are dropped and simply computed again.
     - `world/gen/overworld.rs`: `density` is now `density_grid(x, z, n, s, clim)` (stride `s`, any `n`); `density` calls it with `n` = 5, `s` = 1.0, where every `* 1.0` and `/ 1.0` is exact, so the golden terrain test must still pass. `lod_columns` finds the top of the ground per cell from that grid (first node with density > 0 from the top, zero crossing by linear interpolation as in `generate_terrain`), sea level water/ice, the beach band by `noise_sand`, snow block when cold. `world/gen/chunk_manager.rs`: `climate(x, z, w, d, step)`, `load_block_generator_data` is `climate(.., 1.0)`.
